E10.text:
\begin{proposition}\label{r2:ab-cut-outcome}
If $X=\{a,b,w\}$, then $G$ is isomorphic to one of $\mathfrak T_{56},\mathfrak T_{72},\mathfrak T_{73}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
We distinguish whether $c,d$ lie in different components of $B-X$ or in the same component. Lemmas~\ref{r2:ab-cut-apart} and~\ref{r2:ab-cut-same} give the possible forms of $D_c$, $D_d$ and $D_1$. For each form, discard it if a proper reduction preserves its neighborhood in $X$, the required connectivity and the planarity conditions established for that case. The check in~\supp{E10} leaves $33$ forms for $D_c$, the same forms for $D_d$ after relabeling, and $54$ forms for $D_1$.

R4.text:
\begin{lemma}\label{r2:cellular-center}
Suppose a graph consists of $\Psi_2$, bridges with three attachments among $a,b,c,d$, and possibly $cd$, with $cd$ or a bridge joining $c,d$. If it has a torus embedding in which a closed regular neighborhood of $\Psi_2$ has genus one, then each added bridge $B$ has a planar completion $B+xy+xt$ for some labeling of its attachments.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix such a torus embedding. By Lemma~\ref{lem:torus-complement}, every complementary region of the closed regular neighborhood of $\Psi_2$ is a disk. The edge $cd$ or the bridge joining $c,d$ lies in one such region, so some boundary walk contains both $c$ and $d$. For every genus-one rotation of $\Psi_2$ with such a walk, the calculation in~\supp{R4} shows that no two attachment labels alternate on the boundary of any face containing at least three distinct labels.

R6.text:
\begin{lemma}\label{r1:exterior-faces}
In a torus embedding of $\Psi_3$, let $\mathcal N$ be a closed regular
neighborhood of $\Psi_3$. If $\mathcal N$ has genus one and a face meets
$b,c,d,e$, that face is a disk with attachment sequence
$a,b,a,d,a,c,a,e$, up to cyclic shift and reversal, and the other
two faces have attachment sequences $bc,de$. If $\mathcal N$ has genus zero, any face
meeting $b,c,d,e$ is an annulus with attachment sequences $bc\mid de$, and
the other three faces are disks with attachment sequences $axay$, $ax'$ and
$ay'$, where $\{x,x'\}=\{b,c\}$ and $\{y,y'\}=\{d,e\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\Psi_3$ has $96$ rotation systems. Their facial walks are recorded in~\supp{R6}. No rotation system of genus zero has a face containing all four of $b,c,d,e$ in its boundary walk. The only pair of facial walks in any rotation system of genus zero whose union contains all four has attachment sequences $bc,de$. Thus, by Lemma~\ref{lem:torus-complement}, a face meeting all four in a torus embedding with this rotation must be an annulus with attachment sequences $bc\mid de$. The other three facial walks of such a rotation have attachment sequences $axay$, $ax'$ and $ay'$, and bound disks by the same lemma. Of the genus-one rotations, the only facial attachment sequence containing all four is $abadacae$, up to cyclic shift and reversal. The other two faces have attachment sequences $bc,de$.
\end{proof}
